@* The task board's filter bar (#112).

   PRESENTATIONAL ONLY — it owns no state. Every control reports through an EventCallback and the
   HOST page (ProjectView) holds the TaskFilterState, narrows its task list, and syncs the URL. That
   split is not stylistic: KanbanBoard became generic in PR #300 (`<TItem, TStatus>`) and is shared
   with /ideas, so the filter cannot live on the board — it has to be applied at the call site that
   knows what a TaskItem is. See TaskFilter.cs for why that is also the SAFE place.

   ⚠️ THE TEXT BOXES RENDER FROM LOCAL FIELDS, NOT FROM `Filter`, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING.
   `value="@Filter.Search"` looks obviously right and it CORRUPTS TYPING on a Server circuit.
   Measured on this page before the fix: typing "KeepAlphaBetaGamma" with no inter-key delay left
   "KepAlphaBetaGmma" in the box — two characters gone — and a caret placed mid-word jumped to the
   end. The reasoning that it was safe ("the re-rendered value always equals what the user just
   typed") is wrong, because the render is not synchronous: the keystroke goes to the SERVER, and by
   the time its re-render arrives carrying value="Ke", the user has already typed "Kep" into the DOM.
   The diff then writes the stale "Ke" back over it and the 'p' is gone. `@bind:event="oninput"` has
   the identical defect — the bug is re-rendering the attribute at all, not which directive does it.

   So the attribute must NOT change while the user types. `searchValue`/`assigneeValue`/`claimedByValue`
   are written ONLY when the host changes the text from outside this bar (a URL/deep-link load, or
   Clear), which the host signals by bumping `TextResetToken`. During typing the rendered attribute is
   byte-identical render after render, the diff touches nothing, and the DOM stays the user's.
   The filter itself still updates on EVERY keystroke — the board narrows as you type (AC 2); it is
   only the write-back that stops.

   …and that is exactly why each box is `@key`-ed on `TextResetToken`. Not keeping the DOM in sync
   cuts BOTH ways: on Clear the rendered attribute goes from "" back to "" — no change — so the diff
   would again write nothing and the typed text would sit there while the board showed every card.
   (Measured: "Clear did not empty the search box — it still reads abcXYZdef".) A changed @key
   discards the element and builds a fresh one, which is the one operation guaranteed to put the new
   value in the DOM. It fires only on an external reset, never on a keystroke.

   The host must resolve its filtered list on CHANGE, never in a render-path computed property —
   such a property re-runs on every render, i.e. once per keystroke. *@

<section class="board-filter-bar border-bottom bg-white px-3 py-2"
         data-testid="board-filter-bar"
         aria-label="Filter board tasks">
    <div class="row g-2 align-items-center">
        <div class="col-12 col-md-4">
            <label class="visually-hidden" for="board-filter-search">Search tasks by name or description</label>
            <input id="board-filter-search"
                   @key="@($"search-{TextResetToken}")"
                   type="search"
                   class="form-control form-control-sm"
                   placeholder="Search name or description..."
                   data-testid="board-filter-search"
                   value="@searchValue"
                   @oninput="OnSearchInput" />
        </div>

        <div class="col-6 col-md-2">
            <label class="visually-hidden" for="board-filter-assignee">Filter by assignee</label>
            <input id="board-filter-assignee"
                   @key="@($"assignee-{TextResetToken}")"
                   type="text"
                   class="form-control form-control-sm"
                   placeholder="Assignee..."
                   data-testid="board-filter-assignee"
                   value="@assigneeValue"
                   @oninput="OnAssigneeInput" />
        </div>

        <div class="col-6 col-md-2">
            @* "Filter by claimed by" is what the field is called, and it is gibberish read aloud —
               this label IS the control's accessible name, so it has to be a sentence, not a column
               name. The visible placeholder keeps the domain term ("Claimed by..."). *@
            <label class="visually-hidden" for="board-filter-claimed-by">Filter by who claimed the task</label>
            <input id="board-filter-claimed-by"
                   @key="@($"claimed-by-{TextResetToken}")"
                   type="text"
                   class="form-control form-control-sm"
                   placeholder="Claimed by..."
                   data-testid="board-filter-claimed-by"
                   value="@claimedByValue"
                   @oninput="OnClaimedByInput" />
        </div>

        @* Priority multiselect. Real <input type="checkbox"> elements, not a <select multiple>: a
           multiple-select is unusable with a touch keyboard and gives no at-a-glance read of what is
           on. Bootstrap's btn-check renders them as a segmented control while keeping the checkbox
           semantics (and therefore the keyboard and screen-reader behaviour) intact — the LABEL is
           the hit target, which is what a user (and an e2e test) clicks. *@
        <div class="col-12 col-md-4 d-flex flex-wrap align-items-center gap-2">
            @* A flex row of `btn-check` pairs, NOT a `.btn-group`. Four chips do not fit beside the
               hide-done switch at 390px, and a btn-group that wraps draws its rounded end-caps in
               the middle of the row. Bootstrap's `.btn-check:checked + .btn` rule is an ADJACENT
               SIBLING selector, so it works here exactly as it does inside a group — the input must
               simply stay immediately before its label. *@
            <div class="board-filter-priorities d-flex flex-wrap gap-1" role="group" aria-label="Filter by priority">
                @* @key on BOTH elements, not on a wrapper: the pair must stay ADJACENT SIBLINGS or
                   Bootstrap's `.btn-check:checked + .btn` stops matching and a selected chip loses
                   its fill. Keying each element separately preserves the flat structure while still
                   making the diff identity-based, so a future change that filters or reorders the
                   chips cannot pair an input with another priority's label. (CodeRabbit, PR #362.) *@
                @foreach (var priority in Enum.GetValues<TaskPriority>())
                {
                    var isOn = Filter.Priorities.Contains(priority);
                    <input type="checkbox"
                           @key="@($"priority-input-{priority}")"
                           class="btn-check"
                           autocomplete="off"
                           id="@($"board-filter-priority-{priority}")"
                           checked="@isOn"
                           @onchange="e => OnPriorityToggled.InvokeAsync((priority, e.Value is true))" />
                    <label class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary"
                           @key="@($"priority-label-{priority}")"
                           for="@($"board-filter-priority-{priority}")"
                           data-testid="@($"board-filter-priority-{priority}")">@priority</label>
                }
            </div>

            <div class="form-check form-switch mb-0">
                <input class="form-check-input"
                       type="checkbox"
                       role="switch"
                       id="board-filter-hide-done"
                       data-testid="board-filter-hide-done"
                       checked="@Filter.HideDone"
                       @onchange="e => OnHideDoneChanged.InvokeAsync(e.Value is true)" />
                <label class="form-check-label small" for="board-filter-hide-done">Hide done</label>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="d-flex flex-wrap align-items-center gap-2 mt-2">
        @* Announced politely, not assertively: narrowing a board is not an emergency, and with
           `oninput` binding this text changes on every keystroke. *@
        <span class="text-muted small" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-testid="board-filter-summary">
            @if (Filter.IsActive)
            {
                @($"Showing {MatchCount} of {TotalCount} task{(TotalCount == 1 ? "" : "s")}")
            }
            else
            {
                @($"{TotalCount} task{(TotalCount == 1 ? "" : "s")}")
            }
        </span>

        @* Always rendered, never `@if`-ed: an `@if` would make the bar's second row change height
           the moment the first filter is applied, shoving the whole board down under the user's
           cursor. Disabled rather than hidden, and the disabling IS a focus change — a disabled
           element is dropped from the tab order and a focused one blurs to <body> — but that only
           happens on Clear, where focus leaving a button that now does nothing is the correct
           outcome. (Copilot, PR #362.) *@
        <button type="button"
                class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary ms-auto"
                data-testid="board-filter-clear"
                disabled="@(!Filter.IsActive)"
                @onclick="() => OnClear.InvokeAsync()">
            Clear filters
        </button>
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    /// <summary>The current filter. The host owns it; this component only renders it.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public TaskFilterState Filter { get; set; } = TaskFilterState.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// Bumped by the host whenever it changes the TEXT fields from outside this bar — a URL/deep-link
    /// load, or Clear. That is the ONLY signal that makes this component rewrite the three inputs'
    /// rendered values; see the ⚠️ note at the top for why rewriting them on any other render
    /// silently eats the user's keystrokes.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int TextResetToken { get; set; }

    /// <summary>How many tasks survive <see cref="Filter"/>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int MatchCount { get; set; }

    /// <summary>How many tasks the project has in total.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int TotalCount { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised on every keystroke in the search box.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string?> OnSearchChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised on every keystroke in the assignee box.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string?> OnAssigneeChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised on every keystroke in the claimed-by box.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string?> OnClaimedByChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised when a priority chip is switched on (<c>true</c>) or off (<c>false</c>).</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<(TaskPriority Priority, bool Selected)> OnPriorityToggled { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised when the hide-done switch is flipped.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> OnHideDoneChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised when "Clear filters" is pressed.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnClear { get; set; }

    // What the three text inputs actually RENDER. Never touched by typing — only by an external
    // reset (see TextResetToken). `-1` guarantees the first OnParametersSet adopts, so a deep link
    // populates the boxes on the very first render.
    private string? searchValue;
    private string? assigneeValue;
    private string? claimedByValue;
    private int adoptedTextResetToken = -1;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        if (adoptedTextResetToken == TextResetToken)
        {
            return;
        }

        adoptedTextResetToken = TextResetToken;
        searchValue = Filter.Search;
        assigneeValue = Filter.Assignee;
        claimedByValue = Filter.ClaimedBy;
    }

    // The handlers deliberately do NOT update the rendered values: the DOM already holds exactly
    // what the user typed, and writing it back is the whole bug. They only report upward.
    private Task OnSearchInput(ChangeEventArgs e) => OnSearchChanged.InvokeAsync(e.Value?.ToString());

    private Task OnAssigneeInput(ChangeEventArgs e) => OnAssigneeChanged.InvokeAsync(e.Value?.ToString());

    private Task OnClaimedByInput(ChangeEventArgs e) => OnClaimedByChanged.InvokeAsync(e.Value?.ToString());
}
